## Universal Stack & Project Adaptation Map

These rules adapt automatically to both **single-app repositories** and **multi-package monorepos**:

| Workspace / Target | Applicable Rules |
| --- | --- |
| Mobile / Client App (Flutter iOS/Android, etc.) | `#1–9` (`mobile-flutter.mdc` + cross-cutting & UI/data rules) |
| Web Frontend (`web/`, `apps/web/`, Next.js/React `src/`) | `#1–4`, `#6–8`, `#10` (`web-frontend.mdc` + cross-cutting & web rules) |
| Shared DB / Backend / Migrations (`supabase/migrations/`, `prisma/`, `migrations/`, `sql/`) | Single source of truth for schema, indexes, and access policies (RLS/RBAC); both clients consume |

- **Platform boundary discipline:** Keep platform targets cleanly separated — do not expand frozen legacy web targets inside a mobile codebase when a dedicated web frontend exists, and never apply mobile widget APIs to React/DOM components (or vice versa).
- **Zero hardcoded credentials:** API URLs, project IDs, and keys must always be loaded from centralized config/environment modules — never hardcoded in features or committed as secrets.

---

## Priority When Rules Overlap

1. **`data-sync-and-security.mdc`** wins for data sync, cache reconciliation, encryption, push/tray hot paths, and security.
2. **`mandatory-design.mdc`** wins for semantic colors, typography, icons, shared UI primitives, and localization/bidi.
3. **`english-comments-only.mdc`** wins for comment language and where user-facing strings live.
4. **`engineering-quality.mdc`**, **`interaction-ux.mdc`**, and **`architecture-and-state.mdc`** enforce craftsmanship, UX behavior, and architectural separation where not overridden by (1–2).
5. **`mobile-flutter.mdc`** and **`web-frontend.mdc`** govern platform-specific details within their respective apps without cross-bleeding widget/DOM APIs.

